{"title":"Equal Rational Numbers","difficulty":"Hard","statement":"Given two strings `s` and `t` representing non-negative rational numbers, return whether they represent exactly the same number.\n\nEach string has one of these forms: `IntegerPart`, `IntegerPart.NonRepeatingPart`, or `IntegerPart.NonRepeatingPart(RepeatingPart)`. Parentheses denote digits repeated forever. The non-repeating part may be empty, as in `1.` or `1.(9)`.\n\nFor example, `0.1(6)`, `0.1666(6)`, and `0.166(66)` all represent `1/6`. An infinite sequence of nines is interpreted exactly, so `0.(9)` equals `1`.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1\n\n```text\nInput: s = \"0.(52)\", t = \"0.5(25)\"\nOutput: true\n```\n\n### Example 2\n\n```text\nInput: s = \"0.1666(6)\", t = \"0.166(66)\"\nOutput: true\n```\n\n### Example 3\n\n```text\nInput: s = \"0.9(9)\", t = \"1.\"\nOutput: true\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Each part consists only of decimal digits.\n- The integer part has no leading zeros except for zero itself.\n- 1 <= IntegerPart.length <= 4\n- 0 <= NonRepeatingPart.length <= 4\n- 1 <= RepeatingPart.length <= 4 when present.\n- Each string follows one of the three forms above.\n","starterCode":"class Solution:\n    def isRationalEqual(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        pass\n","entryPoint":"Solution().isRationalEqual","parameters":[{"name":"s","type":"str"},{"name":"t","type":"str"}],"returnType":"bool","tests":[{"input":["0.(52)","0.5(25)"],"expected":true},{"input":["0.1666(6)","0.166(66)"],"expected":true},{"input":["0.9(9)","1."],"expected":true},{"input":["1.","1.(0)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1000.7091","1000.7091(0)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1001.(01)","1001.01(0101)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1022.62","1022.62(0)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1043.396","1043.396(0)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1046.(8)","1046.8(88)"],"expected":true},{"input":["105","106"],"expected":false},{"input":["105.8601","105.8601(0)"],"expected":true},{"input":["1050.5859(1577)","1050.5859(1577)"]